{\rtf1\ansi\ansicpg1252\deff0\deflang1033{\fonttbl{\f0\fswiss\fprq2\fcharset0 Arial;}{\f1\froman\fprq2\fcharset0 Times New Roman;}{\f2\fnil\fcharset0 Arial;}}
{\colortbl ;\red0\green0\blue0;}
\viewkind4\uc1\pard\nowidctlpar\f0\fs24 Mayor Trantalis recognized City Auditor Patrick Reilly. City Auditor Reilly summarized the review of the proposed budget and the conclusions and concerns.\par
\pard\nowidctlpar\li2160\f1\par
\pard\nowidctlpar\f0 Regarding the concerns on overtime expenditures, Acting City Manager Cooper pointed out reductions in next year's budget for Fire-Rescue Department overtime due to increased efficiency in service and scheduling. Work continues with the Chief of Police by reviewing overtime statistics monthly, identifying the causes and needs for the overtime, and looking for ways to recover costs. In the Police Department, factors include vacant positions and things affecting scheduling such as FMLA leave, vacations, and other absences. City Auditor Reilly said overtime can also be addressed during collective bargaining. \par
\pard\nowidctlpar\li2160\f1\par
\pard\nowidctlpar\f0 In response to Commissioner Glassman\rquote s question regarding adequate staffing, Police Chief William Schultz pointed out that the department currently has approximately 30 vacancies plus 20-30 sworn individuals out on FMLA leave. The department is undertaking an updated comparison to cities of like size on overtime statistics and the number of sworn staff and will provide the results when complete. In conversations with colleagues around the state, Chief Schultz said it appears that cities that are comparable in size do have more sworn officers.\cf1\f2\par
}

